WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.958
A secret is any value that grants access, proves identity, protects data, signs a message,

2
00:00:05.958 --> 00:00:08.467
or allows one system to trust another.

3
00:00:08.467 --> 00:00:15.209
The obvious examples are passwords, API keys, OAuth tokens, private keys, SSH keys, TLS private keys,

4
00:00:15.209 --> 00:00:22.421
database credentials, cloud access keys, webhook signing secrets, encryption keys, session signing keys,

5
00:00:22.421 --> 00:00:24.695
and service account credentials.

6
00:00:24.695 --> 00:00:30.653
These values need special handling because possession can be enough to perform an action.

7
00:00:30.653 --> 00:00:36.611
Some secrets are easy to recognize because they look random or are labeled as credentials.

8
00:00:36.611 --> 00:00:39.276
Others are hidden inside a larger value.

9
00:00:39.276 --> 00:00:45.234
A database connection string may include a username, password, hostname, database name,

10
00:00:45.234 --> 00:00:47.586
and driver settings all in one line.

11
00:00:47.586 --> 00:00:50.252
A storage URL may include a signed token.

12
00:00:50.252 --> 00:00:56.523
A configuration object may include both harmless settings and a value that authorizes access.

13
00:00:56.523 --> 00:01:02.089
Treating the whole object as ordinary configuration can expose the sensitive part.

14
00:01:02.089 --> 00:01:03.971
Context matters.

15
00:01:03.971 --> 00:01:06.479
A hostname by itself may not be secret.

16
00:01:06.479 --> 00:01:09.067
A public certificate may not be secret.

17
00:01:09.067 --> 00:01:12.202
A username may not be secret in one environment.

18
00:01:12.202 --> 00:01:18.866
But the combination of a host, user, password, database name, and environment can become sensitive.

19
00:01:18.866 --> 00:01:24.275
A token that looks like a harmless random string may allow production deployment.

20
00:01:24.275 --> 00:01:31.566
A private key may be useful only with the matching account, but if that account controls signing or decryption,

21
00:01:31.566 --> 00:01:33.448
the impact can be serious.

22
00:01:33.448 --> 00:01:37.132
Classification helps teams choose the right controls.

23
00:01:37.132 --> 00:01:43.247
One useful approach is to classify by scope, environment, privilege, lifetime, and impact.

24
00:01:43.247 --> 00:01:45.756
Scope asks what the secret can access.

25
00:01:45.756 --> 00:01:52.576
Environment asks whether it belongs to development, test, staging, production, or a shared platform.

26
00:01:52.576 --> 00:02:00.024
Privilege asks whether the secret can read, write, administer, deploy, sign, decrypt, or manage other secrets.

27
00:02:00.024 --> 00:02:05.198
Lifetime asks whether it is long-lived, short-lived, rotating, or one-time.

28
00:02:05.198 --> 00:02:08.412
Impact asks what would happen if it were exposed.

29
00:02:08.412 --> 00:02:13.900
Not every secret needs the same handling, but every secret needs an owner and a plan.

30
00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:20.799
A low-privilege development token may require different response steps than a production signing key.

31
00:02:20.799 --> 00:02:26.051
A short-lived workload identity token may be safer than a permanent shared key.

32
00:02:26.051 --> 00:02:33.969
A certificate private key may require coordination with infrastructure, application owners, and external partners.

33
00:02:33.969 --> 00:02:40.162
These differences are why inventory and classification are practical tools, not paperwork.

34
00:02:40.162 --> 00:02:44.239
When in doubt, treat access-bearing values conservatively.

35
00:02:44.239 --> 00:02:49.883
Use clearly fake examples in training, such as EXAMPLE_API_KEY or REDACTED_SECRET,

36
00:02:49.883 --> 00:02:57.017
and avoid putting realistic-looking tokens into documents, tests, screenshots, or sample repositories.

37
00:02:57.017 --> 00:03:02.897
The habit you want is simple: if a value could let a person or system cross a trust boundary,

38
00:03:02.897 --> 00:03:05.876
handle it as a secret until proven otherwise.
